{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795561920,"nonce":"5CVCAwIpj4hmUk45K9zlCRJBv84=","title":"A notch border draws darker and grainy compared with the same border on a rect","message":"A notch with `.border(1.0, ThemeState color Border)` in the light theme draws a ring that is darker than a div with the same radius and border, and uneven around the curves: neighbouring pixels alternate in strength.\n\nCause: the native notch fragment shader mixed the border into the fill with `mix(fill_color, prim.border_color, blend)`. The light theme's border token is translucent, so its RGB went in at full weight wherever the ring's coverage reached 1, and only its alpha was weighted. The rect shader composites the border over the fill by the border colour's alpha (premultiplied, then divided back out).\n\nFound while building the rich text editor's floating toolbar as a notch (26002b452b75be9fe98ed2910cd304adffc0646930cfabb9a4f22c9e719c598b). Compared at 1x: a 80x40 div with `rounded(18)` and the notch with the same radius and a top peak, side by side, both with the Border token.\n\nFix: port the rect shader's alpha-aware composition into `sdf_notch.rs`. The ABI branch (`notch_info.x \u003e 0`) still uses `mix` and is left for its own check.","files":null}]}